WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.640
It might come as a surprise to find that&nbsp;
there are a whole bunch of free movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.880
available to watch on YouTube — and it&nbsp;
might come as an even bigger surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.601
to find that some of them are actually&nbsp;
great. Here are a few of the very best.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.440
In 1986, John Carpenter broke the cinematic&nbsp;
mold with Big Trouble in Little China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.480
a satirical kung fu movie that sets Kurt&nbsp;
Russell down in a labyrinthine Chinatown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.440
and leads him through an intriguing plot&nbsp;
involving kidnapping and sorcery. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.440
Big Trouble in Little China bombed at&nbsp;
the box office on release, making back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.382
only around $11.1 million of its budget, which&nbsp;
has been estimated at roughly $20 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.202
In fact, it bombed so badly that it drove&nbsp;
Carpenter away from Hollywood altogether.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.200
But over the years, the film's reputation has&nbsp;
increased, and it's now considered a bona fide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.120
cult classic. Empire magazine rated it&nbsp;
among the top 500 movies of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.960
and many of its vocal defenders praise it for its&nbsp;
skewering of stereotypical Asian roles in movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.160
At the time, it was rare for a Western movie&nbsp;
even to feature a predominantly Asian cast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.081
As actor James Hong has said:

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.640
"There will never be another Big Trouble&nbsp;
in Little China. [...] Martial artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.721
the greatest of all, actors, writers, [in]&nbsp;
that movie, John gave us all a chance."

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.280
There's never a bad time to watch The&nbsp;
Terminator, James Cameron's spectacular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.480
1984 sci-fi action movie about time&nbsp;
travel, killer robots, and romance.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.280
In case you somehow don't know the premise,&nbsp;
Sarah Connor is an ordinary young woman whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.040
life is suddenly upended when she turns out to be&nbsp;
the target of a Terminator - a cyborg sent from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.400
the future to destroy her before she can sire the&nbsp;
son who will lead humanity against the machines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.680
Luckily for her, someone else has&nbsp;
come from the future: Kyle Reese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.680
a soldier sent to protect both Sarah&nbsp;
and humanity's hope for victory.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.621
"Come with me if you wanna live."

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.320
Groundbreaking, intense,&nbsp;
and incredibly fun to boot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.480
The Terminator fully deserves the praise&nbsp;
heaped on it by Variety, who called it:

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:07.600
"A blazing, cinematic comic book, full of&nbsp;
virtuoso moviemaking, terrific momentum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.400
solid performances and a compelling story."

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.080
And the best part of all? Once you're&nbsp;
done with this one, you'll have a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.240
franchise of Terminator movies, shows,&nbsp;
comics, and video games to explore.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.640
Author Walter Tevis' stories are having a bit of&nbsp;
a renaissance these days, mostly thanks to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.640
breakout success of the wildly successful Netflix&nbsp;
adaptation of his novel The Queen's Gambit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:37.120
So now's as good a time as any to enjoy an equally&nbsp;
powerful adaptation of one of his other stories.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.160
Martin Scorsese's The Color of Money is&nbsp;
a "25 years later" sequel to The Hustler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.720
with Paul Newman reprising his role from&nbsp;
the original. Newman plays Fast Eddie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.440
a former pool hall hustler who starts&nbsp;
mentoring Vincent, an up-and-coming player.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.560
There's no way to do a belated sequel and please&nbsp;
everybody, even if you're Martin Scorsese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.960
but this is still the film that finally&nbsp;
got Paul Newman his well-deserved Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.560
Actor win at the Oscars. The cast is the&nbsp;
strongest selling point here: Sheila Benson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.920
writing for the Los Angeles Times,&nbsp;
describes the core players a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.880
quote, "an electrifying unholy trio."&nbsp;
Throw in a great soundtrack and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.160
typically brilliant direction from Scorcese, and&nbsp;
you've got the recipe for a genuine must-watch.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.160
Let Me In is the English-language version&nbsp;
of the Swedish movie Let the Right One In,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.600
and it actually achieves the incredibly difficult&nbsp;
task of living up to the original. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.840
plenty of critics even argue that Let Me In is the&nbsp;
better film. Horror site Bloody Disgusting said:

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.000
"[Director Matt] Reeves takes a bold (and&nbsp;
dangerous) step in (basically) doing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.480
shot-for-shot remake of the Swedish vampire&nbsp;
flick, but finds a way to season it with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.760
little more emotion and flavor. Yes, I&nbsp;
said it, Let Me In is a BETTER movie."

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.280
Meanwhile, Stephen King called it&nbsp;
the best horror film of the decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.680
and Rolling Stone's Peter Travers said:

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.240
"Prepare to be wowed. It's a spellbinder."

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.960
High praise all around. So what's it all about?&nbsp;
Well, Let Me In is a quiet, unsettling study of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.520
the friendship that develops between Owen, a&nbsp;
bullied young boy, and an otherworldly young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.640
girl named Abby. Things get much creepier and much&nbsp;
darker from there, but it'd be a shame to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:22.640
away too much. All you need to know is that Let&nbsp;
Me In is beautifully made and basically impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.859
to look away from - and more than justifies&nbsp;
all the praise it attracted from critics.

00:04:27.859 --> 00:04:33.280
Tune into the beginning of an epic franchise&nbsp;
with the original Stargate, the 1994 sci-fi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.720
film starring Kurt Russell and James Spader. This&nbsp;
movie would eventually result in a sprawling set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.640
of TV series, which ran for a combined total of&nbsp;
17 seasons, and a whole host of tie-in novels.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:50.160
But it all started out relatively modestly, with a&nbsp;
standalone film about an expedition going through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.400
a Stargate to a distant planet, discovering aliens&nbsp;
who once came to Earth and posed as Egyptian gods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.040
Spader plays Daniel Jackson, a civilian&nbsp;
linguist, and Russell is Colonel Jack O'Neil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.440
an Air Force officer who is more than&nbsp;
okay with this being a suicide mission.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.560
The film got a rocky critical&nbsp;
reception on release.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.240
In fact, Roger Ebert hated it so much that&nbsp;
he included it in a 2005 round-up of his most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.920
hated films. But it got a few kinder notices&nbsp;
too, including one review from MovieLine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.280
which described it as, quote, "an instant&nbsp;
camp classic"; that is, the good kind of bad.

00:05:27.318 --> 00:05:28.521
"How ya doing?"

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.840
And of course, at the time no one could&nbsp;
see the kind of mark it would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.760
leave on the TV landscape. It really&nbsp;
is the little sci-fi film that could.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.000
2008's Slumdog Millionaire probably needs&nbsp;
no introduction. Featuring a star director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.440
in Danny Boyle and a star lead in Dev&nbsp;
Patel, this movie was a huge success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.960
first on the festival circuit and then&nbsp;
at the box office. It even went on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.520
win an incredible eight Academy Awards,&nbsp;
including Best Picture and Best Director.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.720
And, of course, it has a terrific hook. The&nbsp;
film tells the life story of Jamal Malik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.160
a poor young man who has had&nbsp;
an unexpected and thrilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.120
victory on the Indian version of&nbsp;
Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:16.720
not missing a single question. Now he has to tell&nbsp;
his life story to prove he came by the answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:22.800
honestly. Described by CinemaBlend as a story of&nbsp;
coincidence, luck, and eventually fate, Slumdog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:28.401
Millionaire isn't so much a strict retelling of&nbsp;
events as it is, as the Los Angeles Times says:

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.320
"A Hollywood-style romantic&nbsp;
melodrama that delivers major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.120
studio satisfactions in an ultra-modern way."

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.240
Of course, the film isn't above criticism,&nbsp;
especially in India - a country which some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.120
have argued the movie misrepresents. Whether&nbsp;
you fall for the magic or just roll your eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.300
however, Slumdog Millionaire is still bound&nbsp;
to be a heck of a conversation-starter.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:56.240
The 2004 documentary Super Size Me chronicles&nbsp;
Morgan Spurlock's ambitious - and unusual - quest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.920
to eat nothing but McDonald's for every meal for&nbsp;
a month, in order to better understand the effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.541
of fast food on the human body. And it should&nbsp;
go without saying, but: Don't try this at home.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.080
Still, as much as no one wants to see&nbsp;
Spurlock vomiting in a parking lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.000
Super Size Me is funny, riveting,&nbsp;
and thought-provoking, to boot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.400
It's very easy to take issue with the validity&nbsp;
of Spurlock's experiment, but it's hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:25.141
argue with the hugely entertaining film&nbsp;
that results. As Empire magazine says:

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:31.200
"It's a hugely enjoyable descent into epic&nbsp;
gluttony. It's the thinking man's Jackass."

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.320
Moulin Rouge! is wonderfully&nbsp;
and unabashedly overblown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:41.920
from its swoon-worthy romance to its gorgeously&nbsp;
artificial sets and its exuberant musical numbers.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:46.560
The story follows Christian, an aspiring&nbsp;
young writer living in a garret in Paris.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.680
When he meets Satine, a courtesan and star&nbsp;
performer at the Moulin Rouge cabaret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:57.280
the two fall in love at first sight. But Satine&nbsp;
needs to be practical - she's promised to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:01.920
weaselly Duke, who has invested heavily in the&nbsp;
Moulin Rouge's new show... which Christian is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:08.240
supposed to write. It's a tangled web of desire,&nbsp;
secrets, danger, heartbreak, and can-cans.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.960
In the movie itself, the artists of the Moulin&nbsp;
Rouge promise that the new show will leave its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.760
audience "dumb with wonderment," and that's&nbsp;
exactly the effect of Moulin Rouge! itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:20.320
Entertainment Weekly sums it up well, saying:

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.080
"Baz Luhrmann's trippy pop&nbsp;
culture pastiche from 2001&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:28.320
[is] an aesthetically arresting ode&nbsp;
to poetry, passion, and Elton John."

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.960
If that sounds at all like&nbsp;
something you would like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.760
well, then - Moulin Rouge!&nbsp;
was pretty much made for you.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:38.880
Even if you haven't seen Ghost in the Shell,&nbsp;
you've probably seen Ghost in the Shell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.320
This cyberpunk noir anime, centering on a&nbsp;
cyborg government agent investigating a hacker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.760
has exerted a huge influence&nbsp;
on science fiction movies.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.240
For example, when the Wachowskis saw it,&nbsp;
their pitch for The Matrix finally gelled:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:57.280
they screened the movie for prospective producers&nbsp;
and announced that they wanted to do it for real.

00:08:58.474 --> 00:08:59.474
“Woah.”

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.880
James Cameron, who is also one of the film's&nbsp;
many admirers, borrowed a little from it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:10.000
making Avatar, while Steven Spielberg did&nbsp;
the same with AI: Artificial Intelligence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:16.000
It really was a perfect storm of stunning visuals,&nbsp;
high-concept ideas, and futuristic speculation.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.920
And, as one of the breakout anime movies&nbsp;
in the West, it remains a terrific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.382
gateway into the form. Reel Rundown gives&nbsp;
it an enthusiastic recommendation, saying:

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:30.480
"As deep as its wonderful animation and&nbsp;
posing lots of philosophical questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:35.360
Ghost in the Shell is a world-class piece of&nbsp;
science fiction that is uniquely Japanese and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.560
utterly irresistible... dark, brooding and adult."

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:43.600
If you've ever found yourself curious about&nbsp;
anime, there's no better place to start than this.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:49.920
2008's Traitor is a gripping, tense thriller&nbsp;
that, thanks to its casting of Don Cheadle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.960
Guy Pearce, now has the benefit of feeling&nbsp;
like a very weird Iron Man 3 reunion.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:59.760
The movie dives straight into a thicket of&nbsp;
moral dilemmas and high-stakes conflicts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:05.840
Cheadle plays Samir Horn, a Sudanese-American man&nbsp;
who seems to be engaging in some murky dealings;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:12.400
so naturally FBI Agent Roy Clayton suspects him of&nbsp;
terrorism. But this is more than a cat and mouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.960
game: It's really a kind of character study&nbsp;
that acts as a vehicle for Cheadle's superb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:23.120
performance. Even critics who found the politics&nbsp;
clumsily handled or the twists implausible agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.920
that it's hard to look away from the movie's&nbsp;
star. A.O. Scott of The New York Times wrote:

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:31.760
"Cheadle's performance gives Traitor&nbsp;
a sense of ethical gravity and real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:35.920
intrigue. [...] Somehow the character&nbsp;
retains his credibility even as the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:40.800
perhaps inevitably, trips over some of&nbsp;
its own complexities and confusions."

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.520
If you can allow for a slightly messy plot&nbsp;
as long as a film is ambitious enough - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.160
has a damn fine lead performance - then&nbsp;
Traitor is bound to glue you to your seat.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:56.000
Sometimes cult classics are weird, challenging&nbsp;
movies that could never have found a mainstream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.920
audience… and sometimes they're adorably silly&nbsp;
rom-com sports movies. Take The Cutting Edge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.960
for example; the endearing early '90s movie&nbsp;
about a prima donna figure and the former ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:11.920
hockey star who becomes her new skating partner.&nbsp;
The movie has plenty of loyal fans who are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:16.800
than capable of summing up its sunny appeal.&nbsp;
In their review for the movie, Decider wrote:

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.680
"At its core, The Cutting Edge is an endlessly&nbsp;
rewatchable, immensely enjoyable film that not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:26.720
only understands but embraces the fact&nbsp;
that rom-coms are supposed to be fun."

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:29.120
And Fansided agreed too, saying:

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:33.200
"[The movie is] like a fine wine [...]&nbsp;
[it] has gotten better with age."

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:38.160
And sure, it's not exactly Citizen Kane -&nbsp;
but not every movie has to be a classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:41.920
Sometimes all you need is a little&nbsp;
romance... and a whole lot of cheese.

